// flesh.cpp -- FleshCore. The mesh engine for playable-race bodies.
//
// WHAT THIS REPLACES. Skyrim treats a body as an indivisible skinned mesh and
// dismemberment mods work around that by hiding bones and dropping pre-made
// props next to the corpse. Every one of those props was authored in advance,
// so it is always the wrong body, in the wrong armour, at the wrong weight.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES INSTEAD. Every playable-race body is treated as a FIELD OF
// CELLS -- a lattice in the body's own bind space, with each triangle of each
// shape they are wearing assigned to the cell it sits in (lattice.h). Nothing
// about the body is authored, named or pre-decided. A wound is a region of
// SPACE: it grows outward through the flesh from wherever the damage landed,
// takes whatever cells it reaches, and those cells become physical objects
// forged on the spot out of their own vertices, their own textures, their own
// armour (chunknif.h). What is left behind stops being drawn, on them alone.
//
// THE ORDER OF OPERATIONS, once per separation:
//   1. VOXELISE, if this body has not been voxelised yet. Read their assembled
//      geometry out of the live scene graph and build their lattice. Cached: a
//      body is voxelised once, again only when its 3D changes, and remembers everything it has lost.
//   2. GROW the wound from the impact point, through cells that actually
//      touch, and split it into islands.
//   3. FORGE one NIF per island and repoint a pool record at each.
//   4. REMOVE those triangles from the actor: a per-actor clone of the shared
//      partition, a private GPU index buffer, the draw count on the clone. No
//      bone is ever scaled -- scaling bones is what tears skin between
//      partial weights and produces the stretched-corpse artifact.
//   5. Write the result line; the script side places the pieces.
//
// THE SHARING LAW governs step 4 and is not negotiable: NiSkinPartition and
// everything under it is SHARED between every actor using that mesh. Writing
// to it directly mutilates strangers. Per-actor and safe: NiAVObject::flags,
// node transforms, scene-graph structure, and the actor's own NiSkinInstance,
// which is where the clone is hung.
//
// Every offset used here is in facts.h with its provenance. Every pointer is
// validated before it is read, and validated for write before it is written.
#ifndef NOMINMAX
#define NOMINMAX
#endif
